\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces The distribution of distance from points uniformly sampled in unit cubes of different dimensions. The vertical red lines indicate the average distance between points. It is clear that as the dimensionality grows, the distribution of distance becomes more concentrated.}}{27}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces The distribution of distance from points uniformly sampled in unit spheres of different dimensions. The vertical red lines indicate the average distance between points. It is clear that as the dimensionality grows, the distribution of distance concentrates towards the length of radius.}}{28}{figure.caption.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.3}{\ignorespaces Part of data processing pipelines introduced in \cite {StegleRNA2015}, with dimension reduction (PCA) being one of the key steps (covariance matrix and visualization).}}{30}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.4}{\ignorespaces The famous 2-dimensional color wheel, created by MDS. It effectively and intuitively captured the similarity and discrepancies between different colors on the spectrum of visible light.}}{33}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.5}{\ignorespaces The input Swiss roll (left) and the output of Isomap (right). The Isomap successfully captures the nature of the manifold and ``unrolls" the Swiss roll.}}{37}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.6}{\ignorespaces A failed LLE output based on the same Swiss roll input in figure \ref {fig:isomap_swissroll}. The algorithm tries to search for neighbors in a overly broad area, causing the result to be distorted.}}{38}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.7}{\ignorespaces A sample of the MNIST dataset (left) and the output of t-SNE (right). t-SNE successfully grouped the handwriting with different digits together, with some minor imperfections (there are two clusters for the digit 0).}}{44}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.8}{\ignorespaces The running result of different SNE algorithms in \cite {Kobak_2020}, with (A) being the light-tail t-SNE, (B) the normal t-SNE and (C) the heavy-tailed t-SNE. Heavy tailed variants are producing tighter and finer clusters, while the light-tail version produces results similar to the original SNE. ($t$-distribution will converge to Gaussian if $\alpha \to \infty $.)}}{47}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.9}{\ignorespaces A simple example of image segmentation, by B Palac, from Wikimedia Commons. Different objects like the bus, the road and the buildings in the background can be detected and identified by algorithms. This technology is widely used in critical fields like self-driving cars.}}{50}{figure.caption.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.10}{\ignorespaces A Waymo self-driving taxi, operating in downtown San Francisco, by Dllu, from Wikimedia Commons. One can easily spot the giant central hub for sensors on the top of the car, as well as many other additional cameras and radars the taxi relies on to deliver a smooth transportation experience. Waymo uses a mixture of technologies, with computer vision and image segmentation being one of the key component of its smooth operation.}}{51}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.11}{\ignorespaces A medical image segmentation that identifies the tumor, by EmmaWiki2020 from Wikimedia Commons, and a 3D image(s) of Heritage Tabo Gompa by Nevil Zaveri from Flickr. To see the 3D image on the right, focus on the center line of the image pair. A third image will appear, the stereo one.}}{51}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.12}{\ignorespaces An example of GRN, by Min Oh, Jaegyoon Ahn, and Youngmi Yoon, from Wikimedia Commons. Graph structure is crucial in visualizing complex relationships between genes and different types of cancers.}}{53}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.13}{\ignorespaces A illustration about HMM modeling multiple sequence alignment, by EMBL-EBI Train Online, from Wikimedia Commons.}}{55}{figure.caption.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.14}{\ignorespaces An intuitive example of path diagrams with edge weights (contributing factors). The daily activities of a person depend on the transportation and food choices, which further depend on the weather, mood and how hungry the person is. Using the diagram, we can conduct analysis such as estimating the impact of weather on the person's daily activities.}}{58}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.15}{\ignorespaces A path analysis diagram from \cite {10.1093/genetics/6.2.111}. Wright used this diagram to visualize and analyze the relations between two mated individuals and their progeny.}}{59}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.16}{\ignorespaces Besag used MRF to develop the Iterated Condition Modes (ICM) algorithm that is able to restore dirty images. The figure is from his work in 1986 \cite {Besag1986}. As we can see, ICM performs much better than the old maximum-likelihood classifier.}}{64}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.17}{\ignorespaces The diagram illustrating belief propagation in \cite {Kschischang2001}. Belief propagation is a powerful model built upon BN and MRF that are broadly used in information theory and other related fields.}}{65}{figure.caption.25}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.18}{\ignorespaces Figure 3.3 from \cite {koller2009probabilistic}, a simple BN that models the letter graph and SAT scores of students.}}{67}{figure.caption.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.19}{\ignorespaces Figure 3.5 from \cite {koller2009probabilistic} that shows common connecting patterns in BN.}}{68}{figure.caption.27}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.20}{\ignorespaces A pair BNs illustrating the concept of d-separation.}}{70}{figure.caption.28}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.21}{\ignorespaces Two MRFs with different edges (and thus cliques) from figure 4.4, \cite {koller2009probabilistic}.}}{72}{figure.caption.29}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.22}{\ignorespaces An MRF with two indicated independent clusters. The nodes with darker grey in the middle are observed, causing the clusters on the left and right to be independent.}}{74}{figure.caption.30}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.23}{\ignorespaces The original handwritten digits from MNIST (top row) and VAE-generated digits (bottom row), from \cite {VAE2023}. Though some outputs are not entirely natural, all are recognizable and there is no mixing of two different digits.}}{79}{figure.caption.31}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Running time and the percentage of valid structures sampled uniformly at random from solutions of K-SAT problems. {Among all the problem sizes}, \textsc {Nelson}\xspace always generate valid solutions and is the most efficient sampler.}}{160}{figure.caption.44}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Running time, the percentage of valid solutions generated, and rounds of resampling for weighted sample generation of K-SAT solutions. {Among all the problem sizes}, \textsc {Nelson}\xspace scales the best among all approaches and always generates valid solutions. }}{160}{figure.caption.45}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Frequency histograms for the number of resample and the total time of \textsc {Nelson}\xspace method for uniformly sampling visiting paths for vehicle routing problem.}}{164}{figure.caption.50}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Implementation pipeline of the \textsc {Nelson}\xspace -CD algorithm with $m=1$. The proposed \textsc {Nelson}\xspace can be efficiently adapted to a Pytorch-based machine learning library and enforces constraint satisfaction during learning.}}{187}{figure.caption.61}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces {\textbf {(a)} An un-directed graph $G(V,E)$ where the vertices are $V=\{v_1,v_2,v_3,v_4\}$ and the un-directed edges are $E=\{e_1=(v_1,v_2),e_2=(v_1,v_3),e_3=(v_2,v_3),e_4=(v_2,v_4),e_5=(v_3,v_4)\}$. \textbf {(b)} A possible sink-free orientation of the edges in the graph and its matrix representation $x$, where every vertex has at least one outgoing edge. }}}{190}{figure.caption.65}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.6}{\ignorespaces The distribution of resampling steps in the \textsc {Nelson}\xspace and Algorithmic-LLL~\citep {DBLP:journals/jacm/MoserT10}. Both of them get a valid sample within $T_{\mathit {tryouts}}$. \textsc {Nelson}\xspace takes much fewer resamples than Algorithmic-LLL because it resamples all the violated clauses at every iteration while Algorithmic-LLL only resamples one of them.}}{196}{figure.caption.68}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Performance of the SPARFA-M, SPARFA-B, and VarFA algorithms on the synthetic data set with different data sizes. Plots from left to right show comparison on accuracy (ACC), area under curve (AUC) and F1 metrics, respectively. Higher is better for all metrics. VarFA performs similarly to SPARFA-M and SPARFA-B.}}{214}{figure.caption.73}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Training run time comparison among VarFA, SPARFA-M, and SPARFA-B on synthetic data sets of varying sizes. VarFA performs approximate Bayesian inference almost 100x faster than SPARFA-B and is close to the run time of SPARFA-M. Thus, VarFA enables practical and scalable Bayesian inference for very large data sets.}}{217}{figure.caption.77}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Violin plot showing the mean and standard deviation of the estimated skill mastery levels on 10 selected students on the 3rd, 4th and 7th latent skills that VarFA computes. In each sub-figure, bottom and top axises respectively shows student IDs and top axis shows the number of questions each student answered. The more questions a student answers, the tighter the credible interval. (Best viewed in color.)}}{219}{figure.caption.83}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Comparison between the estimated skill mastery levels using VarFA's predictions and using empirical observations for student with ID 110. Even though the two curves show different numeric values, they nevertheless demonstrate similar trends, showing that the predictions reasonably match our intuition about student's skill mastery levels. }}{227}{figure.caption.87}%
